\section{Unified Stability Criterion}

\begin{theorem}[\textbf{Unified Multi-Domain Stability Bound}]
Let $\Delta t$ be the simulation timestep and 
$\lambda_{\max}(\mathcal{J})$ be the largest eigenvalue of the 
Jacobian matrix $\mathcal{J} = \partial \mathcal{U} / \partial \mathbf{X}$.
The unified simulation remains stable under cross-domain coupling if:

\begin{equation}
\Delta t < \frac{2}{|\lambda_{\max}(\mathcal{J})|}.
\end{equation}

Furthermore, if each subsystem satisfies its own stability bound:

\begin{equation}
\Delta t < \min
\left\{
\Delta t_{\text{CFL}},
\Delta t_{\text{GW}},
\Delta t_{\text{crypto}},
\Delta t_{\text{DSP}}
\right\},
\end{equation}

then the unified engine is globally stable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The unified update operator $\mathcal{U}$ is a composition of 
Lipschitz-continuous subsystem operators. 
Stability requires the spectral radius of the Jacobian to remain 
bounded by $2/\Delta t$. 
Applying Gershgorin’s circle theorem across block matrices 
corresponding to each subsystem yields the minimum bound.
\end{proof}

